<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Changing Ecology – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Our Ecology Is Changing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create informative articles on common interests. Interactive: Produce a collaborative story on local or global interest topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create informative articles explaining local ecological changes and their impact on the community (e.g., "If the town plants more trees, the air quality will improve.")
Can create informative articles about environmental conservation guidelines, listing actions to protect local forests (e.g., "If we prevent illegal logging, the forest ecosystem will recover faster.")
Can work with peers to produce a story about ecological changes, incorporating community conservation efforts (e.g., "Over the past few years, our community has implemented several conservation projects that have significantly improved the local ecosystems.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of local forests (some healthy, some affected by deforestation). Students are asked to quickly write down three words or short phrases that come to mind for each image in a "word cloud" format on the board or a digital tool. This activates prior knowledge related to "Our Changing Forests" and prepares them for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple sentence incorporating one of the images and target vocabulary, such as "Deforestation has significantly affected the local biodiversity" or "If we plant more trees, air quality will improve." The teacher explicitly uses the present perfect and first conditional, highlighting their use for describing ecological changes and potential solutions. Pronunciation of "biodiversity" (/baɪoʊdaɪˈvɜːrsɪti/) and word stress in "significantly" is modeled.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of impact does 'deforestation' have on forests? Positive or negative?" (Checks understanding of 'deforestation' and 'impact').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'If we preserve our forests,' am I talking about the past, present, or future action?" (Checks understanding of first conditional function).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students receive a short informative article excerpt (approx. 150-180 words) titled "Panama's Forests: A Call for Conservation." This article describes recent ecological changes, uses present perfect to describe past impacts, first and second conditionals for proposed solutions, and comparatives for different ecosystems. It integrates vocabulary such as 'biodiversity,' 'pollution,' 'logging,' 'conservation,' 'forest health,' 'ecosystems,' 'plastic waste,' 'air quality,' and 'native species.' Emphasis is placed on sentences like, "Deforestation has significantly affected marine life in our rivers" and "If people planted more trees, forests would recover faster." The article also includes phrases reflecting pragmatic functions such as "How has deforestation affected our area?" and sociolinguistic expressions like "Protecting our forests benefits everyone."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand and interpret written information about environmental issues and conservation efforts, identifying key arguments and proposals within informative texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the article silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main problem discussed in this article, and what is its overall message?" Students write a one-sentence answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students answer 3-4 True/False questions based on specific details in the text, e.g., "Illegal logging has reduced biodiversity in our area." (True/False). "If we prevent illegal logging, the forest ecosystem will recover faster." (True/False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases describing ecological changes with phrases describing their proposed solutions from the article, focusing on conditional structures.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice constructing grammatically accurate sentences and short paragraphs using the Present Perfect, First and Second Conditional, and Comparatives, integrating target vocabulary related to environmental issues and conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Sentence Transformation):</b> The teacher provides sentences in the present tense or simple past, and students rewrite them using the Present Perfect to highlight recent impact or first/second conditional for hypothetical situations. E.g., "Logging damages forests." → "Logging <b>has damaged</b> our forests significantly." Or "People don't plant enough trees." → "If people <b>planted</b> more trees, forests <b>would recover</b> faster." (Focus on verbs: impact, reduce, affect, improve, recover).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Gap-fill Paragraphs):</b> Students complete short paragraphs with appropriate vocabulary (deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, conservation, air quality) and correct forms of conditional sentences or present perfect verbs. For example, "Over the last decade, [_______________] (deforestation/pollution) has severely [_______________] (affect/improve) our local [_______________] (ecosystems). If we [_______________] (implement) sustainable [_______________] (logging/conservation) practices, we [_______________] (will see/would see) a significant improvement in [_______________] (forest health/plastic waste)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Sentence Building & Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive sentence starters or prompts (e.g., "If the government...", "Our community has...", "Urban ecosystems..."). Each student writes one sentence incorporating specific grammar (conditional, present perfect, comparative) and at least two target vocabulary words. They then exchange sentences with their partner, check for grammatical accuracy and vocabulary use, and offer suggestions for improvement, focusing on clarity and coherence in their written output. They also pay attention to correct spelling for words with /aɪ/ (life, try) and -ate endings (climate, accurate) if applicable.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Informative Article Writing. Students work in groups of 3-4 to create a short informative article (approx. 100-120 words) for a local community newsletter titled "Protecting Our Local Forests: What We Can Do."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group brainstorms 2-3 significant ecological changes observed in their local area (or a hypothetical Panamanian forest) related to deforestation, pollution, or impacts on biodiversity/rivers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must then propose 2-3 specific conservation actions or guidelines that the community can implement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The article must include:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one sentence using the Present Perfect to describe an existing ecological change (e.g., "Deforestation has significantly reduced native species in our area.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one First Conditional sentence proposing a direct action and its positive outcome (e.g., "If we organize monthly clean-ups, plastic waste in our rivers will reduce.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one Second Conditional sentence discussing a hypothetical positive impact if actions were more widespread (e.g., "If people planted more trees, our air quality would improve dramatically.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one Comparative sentence (e.g., "Local forest health is much healthier compared to five years ago due to community efforts.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A minimum of 5 vocabulary words from the list (e.g., forest, conservation, ecosystems, air quality, reduce, protect, plant, healthier, significant).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Phrases expressing environmental concerns politely and using community-focused language (e.g., "Protecting our forests benefits everyone," "Together, we can make a difference in conservation").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups draft their article, focusing on clear, coherent writing. They are encouraged to use a peer-editing approach within their group.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, collaborative writing, successful incorporation of grammatical structures and vocabulary to complete the communicative task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups finalize and submit their informative article. Each group also conducts a brief peer review of another group's article using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Relevance:</b> Does the article address local ecological changes and conservation? (0-2 pts)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar (Present Perfect, Conditionals, Comparatives):</b> Correct and appropriate use of target grammar structures. (0-3 pts)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Accurate and varied use of target environmental vocabulary. (0-2 pts)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Cohesion:</b> Is the article easy to understand and well-organized? (0-2 pts)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Completion:</b> Does the article meet all specified requirements (word count, inclusion of specific sentence types)? (0-1 pt)</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an individual exit ticket with these prompts:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing informative articles today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am confident in writing sentences using [Present Perfect / First Conditional / Second Conditional / Comparatives] because..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the article did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can effective written communication about environmental issues encourage community action?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this writing lesson? Students demonstrated good understanding of conditional structures when proposing solutions. The collaborative writing fostered peer learning and supported weaker writers. Some groups struggled with integrating comparatives smoothly, needing more explicit modeling. Scaffolding for article organization (introduction, problem, solution, conclusion) could be reinforced for future writing tasks.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their articles and integrate the information into a larger 21st Century Skills Project, possibly a digital presentation or a campaign poster, requiring them to mediate their written work into another format and present it.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>